# 🚨 PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc68lzcU — headers merge: deletions now stick (replace-by-presence)

Fixed in `73f784c496` (`browser_ext/sw/main.js`, `mergeProviderSave`) — exactly the shape you proposed:

```js
// Headers merge by presence over a sanitized map — the SAME contract as
// the Dart settings_put hook: a non-empty incoming set REPLACES the
// stored one (deletions stick), a blank/absent field keeps the stored
// set. An empty merged result omits the key entirely.
const incomingHeaders = sanitizeHeaders(msg?.headers);
const headers = Object.keys(incomingHeaders).length
  ? incomingHeaders
  : sanitizeHeaders(stored?.headers);
```

- Your repro now behaves as specified: stored `{authorization, x-old}` + save with `headers: 'x-new: yes'` → stored is exactly `{x-new: yes}`; `headers: ''` still keeps the stored set (the documented blank-keeps-stored behavior, unchanged).
- The JS writer now has the same merge semantics as `browser_ext/dart/src/settings_merge.dart` (replace-by-presence), so both writers of `faProvider.headers` agree; a deleted `authorization` header no longer rides requests to a new endpoint.
- TDD: added `gh-2425 rework: a save with a reduced header set REPLACES the stored set — deletions stick` to `browser_ext/test/provider_save_test.mjs` — RED against the union (asserted the stored set after the reduced save), GREEN after the fix; the pre-existing `blank header text = keep` test still passes, so the documented keep-behavior is pinned on both sides of the change.
- The stale-parity claims are corrected in place: the block comment above `mergeProviderSave` and the file-top comment now state replace-by-presence, and the CHANGELOG bullet spells it out ("custom headers replace-by-presence so a header line the user deleted actually deletes — a dropped authorization header stops riding requests").
- Suite: `node --test browser_ext/test/*.test.mjs browser_ext/test/*_test.mjs` → 95/95; Dart parity suites (`settings_merge_test`, `providers_sync_test`) → 33/33.

# 💡 PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc68lzfa — storedInt tightened to positive ints (Dart reader parity)

Fixed in `73f784c496` (`browser_ext/sw/main.js`) — picked up with the blocking fix (same file):

```js
/** Positive int | positive-int numeric string → number; non-integral,
 * non-positive, blank/garbled/other → null. Dart reader parity ... */
function storedInt(value) {
  const t = typeof value === 'number' ? String(value) : typeof value === 'string' ? value.trim() : '';
  if (!/^[+-]?\d+$/.test(t)) return null;
  const n = Number(t);
  return n > 0 ? n : null;
}
```

Two deliberate hardenings beyond the suggested snippet, both for exact Dart parity:

1. The **number branch** is held to the same contract (`typeof value === 'number'` paths flow through the same regex + `> 0` check) — the suggested `Number.isInteger(n) && n > 0` guard on the string path alone would still store a number-typed `-5`/`264000.5` payload (v2 protocol shape).
2. The regex (`^[+-]?\d+$`) mirrors `int.tryParse` decimal-digit semantics, so `'1e3'`/`'0x10'`/`'Infinity'` can't slip through `Number()` either.

- Save path: `'264000.5'`, `'-5'`, `'0'`, `264000.5`, `-5`, `0` all fall back to the stored window (Dart `storedIntSetting` int-parses / `windowSettingsFromStored` drops `<= 0` — the override either applies as a positive int or never silently saves).
- Read path: `provider.get` now drops a stored double (`264000.5` in storage → answers `0`, the existing no-override encoding) instead of echoing it into the panel — matching the Dart reader that ignores it.
- TDD: two new tests in `browser_ext/test/provider_save_test.mjs` — `non-integral / non-positive contextWindow input falls back to stored` (6 cases × storage + boot-config assertions) and `provider.get drops a non-integral stored window (read-back parity)`; both RED before the fix (e.g. stored `264000.5` echoed back verbatim), GREEN after.
- The existing string-tolerant positive cases (`'264000'`, `' 1000000 '`, garbled→keep, blank→keep) all still pass — 95/95 across `browser_ext/test/`.
